.TH OYVEY 1 "2026-10-05" "oyvey 0.8.4" "Rak package manager and build system"
.SH NAME
oyvey \- Git-based package manager and build system for the Rak language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B oyvey
.I <command>
.RI [ <args> ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B oyvey
is the official package manager and build system for Rak. It resolves GitHub
dependencies into a reproducible lockfile, vendors them where the
.B rakc
compiler can find them, and drives the compiler for
.BR build ,
.BR run ,
and
.BR test .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B new <project>
Generate a new Rak project: a manifest, a
.BR src/main.rak
entry point, a
.B tests/
directory, a
.BR .gitignore ,
and a README. The result is immediately buildable with
.B oyvey build
and runnable with
.BR "oyvey run" .
Use
.B \-\-lib
for a library project.
.TP
.B init
Initialize an existing directory as a project. Writes a manifest and an entry
point if they are missing, and never overwrites either.
.TP
.B add <github-repo>
Add a GitHub package dependency. Clones the repository, reads the package name
from its manifest, records it in
.BR deps ","
and installs it.
.TP
.B remove <package>
Remove a dependency, its vendored copy, and its lockfile entry.
.TP
.B install
Resolve and install every dependency in the manifest, transitively. Writes
.BR oyvey.lock .
Reuses the revisions already recorded there when they still satisfy the
manifest constraints.
.TP
.B update
Re-resolve dependencies within their constraints, picking the newest matching
version, and rewrite
.BR oyvey.lock .
.TP
.B build
Compile the project through
.BR "rakc build" ,
producing a standalone executable.
.TP
.B run
Run the entry point through
.BR "rakc run" .
.TP
.B test
Run the project's tests through
.BR "rakc test" .
.TP
.B clean
Remove the executable produced by
.BR "oyvey build" .
.TP
.B list
List installed packages with their versions and locked revisions.
.TP
.B tree
Print the resolved dependency tree, cycle-safe.
.TP
.B audit
Verify that every package in
.B oyvey.lock
is installed and that its manifest checksum matches. Exits non-zero on a
mismatch.
.TP
.B lock
Resolve dependencies and write
.B oyvey.lock
without vendoring.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.TP
.B help [<command>]
Show help, or help for one command.
.SH DEPENDENCY SPECS
.TP
.B user/repo
Track the default branch.
.TP
.BI user/repo@ ^1.2
Caret constraint: compatible with 1.2.
.TP
.BI user/repo@ ~1.2
Tilde constraint: compatible with 1.2.x.
.TP
.BI user/repo@ 1.2.3
Exact version.
.TP
.BI user/repo# rev
Pin to an exact git revision (tag, branch, or commit). May be combined with a
constraint; the revision wins.
.PP
A spec that is already a URL
.RB ( https:// ", " ssh:// ", " file:// )
is used verbatim, which allows a self-hosted Git server.
.SH MANIFEST
The manifest is a Rak file named
.BR package.rak :
.PP
.RS
.nf
let name = "mylib"
let version = "0.1.0"
let description = "A Rak package"
let license = "MIT"
let entry = "src/main.rak"
let deps = {
    net: "user/rak-net",
    crypto: "user/rak-crypto@^1.0"
}
.fi
.RE
.SH LOCKFILE
.B oyvey.lock
is Cargo-style TOML. Each package records the exact git revision it was built
from and a SHA-256 checksum of its manifest:
.PP
.RS
.nf
version = 1

[[package]]
name = "rak-net"
version = "0.2.1"
source = "git+https://github.com/user/rak-net#0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567"
checksum = "e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855"
.fi
.RE
.SH CACHE
Downloads are kept in a global cache under
.B ~/.oyvey
(override with
.BR OYVEY_HOME ),
as bare clones plus one checkout per locked revision. Installed packages are
vendored into
.B <project>/packages/
so that
.B "import <pkg>"
resolves through the compiler's normal module search.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OYVEY_HOME
Global cache location (default: \fB~/.oyvey\fR).
.TP
.B RAK_PATH
Extra module search path. When invoking the compiler, oyvey prepends
.B <project>/packages
to it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rakc (1)
